--TEST--
`Core\Bytes::compare` answers the ordering `Core\Str::compare` gives the same octets, and is a total order over a swept table — spec § 7, `rule:types/bytes`, `rule:core-api/shape-rules` R20
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `bytes-indexes-and-orders-by-byte-offset.nvst` pins what `compare` answers
// for a handful of written pairs. This case pins the two properties those
// lines cannot show: that the answer is the *same* ordering `Core\Str` gives
// the identical octets, and that the ordering is total — sign, antisymmetry,
// trichotomy and transitivity counted over every pair and every triple of one
// table rather than read off a line. A member that grew its own comparison
// still prints plausibly against any single row.
//
// `crates/nvs-stdlib/src/bytes.rs:588` owns the implementation and its doc
// comment owns why the answer is exactly -1, 0 or 1 with no magnitude.

final class Ord {
    // The two members under test, over the same octets: a `string` is UTF-8,
    // so `$s as bytes` is the identity on the buffer both then read (`rule:types/conversion`
    // ), which is what makes "the same octets" a fact rather than a hope.
    public static function bytes(string $a, string $b): int {
        return Core\Bytes::compare($a as bytes, $b as bytes);
    }

    public static function text(string $a, string $b): int {
        return Core\Str::compare($a, $b);
    }

    // The ordering the table is *written* in, so a member that agrees with
    // `Core\Str` because both are wrong still fails the sweep below.
    public static function sign(int $i, int $j): int {
        if ($i < $j) {
            return -1;
        }
        if ($i > $j) {
            return 1;
        }
        return 0;
    }

    public static function le(int $c): string {
        if ($c <= 0) {
            return "y";
        }
        return "n";
    }
}

// Written in ascending byte order, and chosen for the three shapes an octet
// ordering has to get right: the empty buffer, which is a prefix of everything;
// two buffers where one is a proper prefix of the other ("a", "ab", "abc"); and
// a pair that only an *unsigned* octet comparison orders this way — "ß" is
// 0xc3 0x9f, so it sorts after "z" (0x7a) here and would sort before it if the
// octets were read as signed.
array<string> $table = ["", "A", "a", "ab", "abc", "b", "z", "ß"];
var $n = Core\Arr::count($table);

// The prefix rule and the sign convention, written out once so the counts below
// have something to be a generalization of.
echo Ord::bytes("", "a"), " ", Ord::bytes("ab", "abc"), " ", Ord::bytes("abc", "ab"), " ", Ord::bytes("ab", "ab"), "\n";

// A magnitude is what PHP 8 already removed from `strcmp`, so the answer is one
// of three values however far apart the first differing octets are: "a" and "z"
// are 25 apart and "A" and "ß" are 130, and both answer -1.
echo Ord::bytes("a", "z"), " ", Ord::bytes("A", "ß"), " ", Ord::bytes("z", "a"), "\n";

// 1. Agreement. Every pair, both ways round, asked of both members: an ordering
//    that disagreed on one pair fails the count while still looking right on
//    every line above.
int $agree = 0;
int $i = 0;
int $j = 0;
for ($i = 0; $i < $n; $i = $i + 1) {
    for ($j = 0; $j < $n; $j = $j + 1) {
        if (Ord::bytes($table[$i], $table[$j]) == Ord::text($table[$i], $table[$j])) {
            $agree = $agree + 1;
        }
    }
}
echo $agree, " of ", $n * $n, "\n";

// 2. Totality and trichotomy against the order the table is written in: every
//    pair answers exactly the sign of its two indices, so the sweep asserts the
//    whole ordering and not merely that it is *an* ordering. Equality is the
//    diagonal and nothing else, because the table's entries are distinct.
int $ordered = 0;
int $zeros = 0;
for ($i = 0; $i < $n; $i = $i + 1) {
    for ($j = 0; $j < $n; $j = $j + 1) {
        var $c = Ord::bytes($table[$i], $table[$j]);
        if ($c == Ord::sign($i, $j)) {
            $ordered = $ordered + 1;
        }
        if ($c == 0) {
            $zeros = $zeros + 1;
        }
    }
}
echo $ordered, " of ", $n * $n, ", ", $zeros, " equal\n";

// 3. Antisymmetry, asked of the member alone rather than of the table's order:
//    swapping the arguments negates the answer, for every pair.
int $anti = 0;
for ($i = 0; $i < $n; $i = $i + 1) {
    for ($j = 0; $j < $n; $j = $j + 1) {
        if (Ord::bytes($table[$i], $table[$j]) == 0 - Ord::bytes($table[$j], $table[$i])) {
            $anti = $anti + 1;
        }
    }
}
echo $anti, " of ", $n * $n, "\n";

// 4. Transitivity, over every triple: `a <= b` and `b <= c` implies `a <= c`.
//    This is the property a hand-written comparison loses first, and no pair
//    can show it.
int $triples = 0;
int $transitive = 0;
for ($i = 0; $i < $n; $i = $i + 1) {
    for ($j = 0; $j < $n; $j = $j + 1) {
        for (int $k = 0; $k < $n; $k = $k + 1) {
            $triples = $triples + 1;
            if (Ord::le(Ord::bytes($table[$i], $table[$j])) == "y" && Ord::le(Ord::bytes($table[$j], $table[$k])) == "y") {
                if (Ord::le(Ord::bytes($table[$i], $table[$k])) == "y") {
                    $transitive = $transitive + 1;
                }
            } else {
                $transitive = $transitive + 1;
            }
        }
    }
}
echo $transitive, " of ", $triples, "\n";

// 5. The buffers the table cannot hold, because a `string` is UTF-8 and these
//    are not: `compare` is defined over octets, so a buffer that no `string`
//    can name still orders, and it orders by the same unsigned rule. 0xff is
//    the last octet there is, and it sorts after every ASCII one.
bytes $high = Core\Encoding::fromHex("ff");
bytes $low = Core\Encoding::fromHex("00");
bytes $none = Core\Encoding::fromHex("");
echo Core\Bytes::compare($low, $high), " ", Core\Bytes::compare($high, $low), " ", Core\Bytes::compare($none, $low), " ", Core\Bytes::compare($high, $high), "\n";
--EXPECT--
-1 -1 1 0
-1 -1 1
64 of 64
64 of 64, 8 equal
64 of 64
512 of 512
-1 1 -1 0
